The U.S. state of [[Utah]] first required its residents to register their [[motor vehicle]]s in 1909. Registrants provided their own [[Vehicle registration plate|license plate]]s for display until 1915, when the state began to issue plates.<ref>{{cite web |title=Old Utah License Plates |url=http://leatherlicenseplates.com/old-utah-license-plates-vintage-utah-license-plates/ |publisher=LeatherLicensePlates.com |date=<!-- Deny Citation Bot--> |access-date=June 10, 2017 |archive-date=March 4, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170304112650/http://leatherlicenseplates.com/old-utah-license-plates-vintage-utah-license-plates/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

{{As of|2026}}, plates are issued by the Utah State Tax Commission through its Division of Motor Vehicles. Only rear plates are required for most classes of vehicles, including motorcycles and trailers. The front plate requirement was lifted for passenger vehicles on January 1, 2025.<ref>{{cite web|title=Utah officially changed its license plate requirements - Here's when you can ditch your front plate |url=https://www.abc4.com/news/local-news/utah-officially-changed-its-license-plate-requirements-heres-when-you-can-ditch-your-front-plate/ |publisher=ABC4 |access-date=March 15, 2024}}</ref> Month and year decals have now been consolidated into one sticker instead of two separate decals. New license plates will now be flat-screen printed instead of being embossed.

